Reason:
<text>

The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```
par = fec_read_parity(v, rsb, block_offset, &offset,
		      par_buf_offset, &buf, bio->bi_ioprio);
```
```
for (j = 0; j < v->fec->roots - par_buf_offset; j++)
	par_buf[par_buf_offset + j] = par[offset + j];
```
```
if (offset < v->fec->io_size && (offset + v->fec->roots) > v->fec->io_size) {
	par_buf_offset = v->fec->io_size - offset;
	for (j = 0; j < par_buf_offset; j++)
		par_buf[j] = par[offset + j];
	offset += par_buf_offset;
} else
	par_buf_offset = 0;
```

Reason:
The old `fec_read_parity` function computed the parity block and offset assuming that the requested parity bytes for a single RS codeword fit entirely within one block. The call site then copied `roots - par_buf_offset` bytes from the returned pointer. If the required parity bytes spanned a block boundary (e.g., because `(rsb + block_offset) * roots` has a remainder such that `offset + roots > io_size`), the code attempted to handle the split by saving a partial copy and adjusting `par_buf_offset`. However, this logic failed to correctly handle the case where the split occurs at the very start of a new RS codeword, because the initial read could be from the wrong block. Moreover, the check for a split only considered `offset < io_size && offset + roots > io_size`, but the actual problem is that the parity data is not aligned: the needed bytes may be partially in one block and partially in the next, and `par_buf_offset` from previous iterations could corrupt the indexing. This led to reading beyond the bounds of the parity buffer when the alignment required exactly `roots` bytes that crossed a block boundary.  

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```
parity_block = (rsb + block_offset) * v->fec->roots;
parity_pos = parity_block & (v->fec->io_size - 1);
parity_block >>= v->data_dev_block_bits;
par = dm_bufio_read_with_ioprio(v->fec->bufio, parity_block, &buf,
				bio->bi_ioprio);
```
```
to_copy = min(v->fec->io_size - parity_pos, v->fec->roots);
for (j = 0; j < to_copy; j++)
	par_buf[j] = par[parity_pos++];
if (to_copy < v->fec->roots) {
	parity_block++;
	parity_pos = 0;
	dm_bufio_release(buf);
	par = dm_bufio_read_with_ioprio(v->fec->bufio,
					parity_block, &buf,
					bio->bi_ioprio);
	for (; j < v->fec->roots; j++)
		par_buf[j] = par[parity_pos++];
}
```

Reason:
The fix correctly computes the absolute position in the parity data (`parity_block`) and then extracts the block index (`parity_block >> v->data_dev_block_bits`) and byte offset within that block (`parity_pos = parity_block & (io_size - 1)`). Since `io_size` is a power of two, the modulo is computed via a bitwise AND, and the initial read is always from the correct block. Then, it copies up to `roots` parity bytes into `par_buf` using the pattern: first copy as many bytes as remain in the current block (`to_copy = min(io_size - parity_pos, roots)`), and if that is less than the total needed, it reads the next block and copies the rest. This transparently handles any split across block boundaries without requiring a separate `par_buf_offset` tracking variable, and eliminates the possibility of out-of-bounds reads because the copy count is always limited by the available bytes in the current block before reading the next block.

